  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "A line passes through the point $(2,6)$ and crosses the $y$-axis at the point $(0,4)$. At what point does it cross the $x$-axis?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(-4,0)$",
      "worked": "First find the gradient:\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{6-4}{2-0}\\\\m&=1\\end{align*}$$\nLet the point where the line crosses the $x$-axis be $(a,0)$. Using $(0,4)$ and $(a,0)$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{0-4}{a-0}&=1\\\\\\frac{-4}{a}&=1\\\\a&=-4\\end{align*}$$\nThe line crosses the $x$-axis at $(-4,0)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
